00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:03.830 align:start position:0%
unless you've been hiding under a rock  you've probably seen or at least heard  of bird box a conceptual  post-apocalyptic thriller from Netflix  has taken social media by storm sparking  internet memes jokes and even a  potentially dangerous social media  challenge for many viewers the most  interesting aspect of the movie involved  the creatures in the film monsters that  either drive people to kill themselves  or to become fanatics who kill on their  behalf  the mysterious creatures drive people  mad on sight but in the finished movie  they go completely unseen neither bird  box nor the book it's based on ever show  the creatures in physical form only  adding to their mystery now thanks to  creature designer and sculptor Andy Berg  Holtz and special effects studio SFX  Atlas we finally know what bird boxes  monsters would have looked like on the  screen had plans for the movie gone  differently Berg Holtz who is credited  as the special effects makeup lead for  bird box took to Instagram in early  January 2019 to share several pieces of  concept art together the pictures work  to reveal the creature design that the  filmmakers almost used in the final  movie according to Berg Holtz the  intention for the movie was for no two  people to see the creatures in the same  way with their physical forms changing  depending on who is looking at them the  design featured in the photos he shared  is how one of the creatures would have  looked to Sandra Bullock's character  Mallory  the images show a baby-faced monster  with a bulbous vein covered head swollen  ears and a snake-like body meant to be  created using CGI reportedly the  creature is what Mallory would have seen  in a nightmarish dream sequence that was  ultimately scrapped from the final cut  as berg Holtz described it the  custom-made creature design was inspired  by Mallory's pregnancy playing on the  anxieties and fears of an expectant  mother he wrote on Instagram  the producers felt Sandra's nightmare  would have something to do with a  twisted demonic baby creature attacking  her Burr Colts said he designed several  different takes on the monster design  before settling on the final product he  wrote I sculpted at least 3 or 4  variations on the design before it was  approved which began as a more  aggressive monster II look and was  revised to be a bit more subtle in the  end after the release of bird box the  company SFX Atlas also took to Instagram  to share with the monster design might  have  looked like inaction the company's post  shows special-effects artists Stephen  Prouty applying prosthetics and makeup  to actor Dirk Rogers who would have  stood in for the creature during the  shooting of the nightmare sequence  allegedly not everyone who helped create  the designs for the monsters is happy  that their work ended up getting cut  from the movie especially after the film  proved to be so widely seen Howard  burger the co-owner of KNB EFX group  where the designs for the bird box  creatures were crafted seemed to air his  complaints in a sense deleted Instagram  post writing it's always a bit  disappointing when so much effort goes  into something that ends up in the  cutting room floor but I get it and it's  always what is best for the final  product when bird box was released some  viewers found it annoying that the movie  never shows what the monsters actually  look like making the unused creature  effects extra interesting but despite  how unsettling the designs appear some  fans have argued that the decision to  keep the monsters off screen was to the  movies benefit letting viewers come up  with their own interpretations of what  lurks beyond the blindfolds Sandra  Bullock seems to feel the same way  speaking in an interview with horror  movie site Bloody Disgusting Bullock  said she couldn't help but laugh at the  creature in action which she described  as quote a long fat baby turns out the  choice of whether to show the creatures  are not held a lot of importance if  viewer found the monsters funny it could  have ruined the whole film director  susanne bier apparently agreed and  pushed to remove the nightmare moment  from the movie as she put it to Bloody  Disgusting  it so easily becomes funny we actually  shot that scene and spent a lot of  energy on it but every time I saw it I  was like this is not going to be tense  it's just going to be funny  now that the movie is out in the wild  people seem to agree that keeping the  monsters off screen was the right choice  as burg Holtz wrote in his Instagram  post I actually really liked the movie  and think it was better off not showing  the makeup kudos to the director for  sticking to her guns on that one check  out one of our newest videos right here  plus even more looper videos about bird  box are coming soon subscribe to our  YouTube channel and hit the bell so you  don't miss a single one  [Music]
 

